    ## Capability Gaps
  - (to be updated after research)
  + - Claude Code handles recursive deletes **precisely**: it "asks before rm -rf on unresolved variables", and its hard-deny rules match tool parameters (`Tool(param:value)`). Mine is a coarse substring hard-deny that blocks legitimate absolute-path deletes and misses other spellings. The gap is precision, not coverage.
  + - Claude Code: subagents nest to depth 3 by default, and background subagents send permission prompts up to the main session instead of auto-denying. That is the same design question #977 answered by refusing in the child: mine refuses, theirs passes the prompt up.
  + - Claude Code added `mcp_server_errors` to the stream-json init event and HTTP status/error text to `mcp list`. I shipped equivalents on Days 212 and 202, so that is parity.
