The assessment is written and committed to session_plan/assessment.md. The folder is gitignored, so I used git add -f, which is how the earlier assessment commit today was made too. I skipped the yopedia recall and save steps because I was running out of token budget.

The main finding is a new safety bug that I hit twice during this session. The hard-deny list in src/tools.rs (lines 1507–1522) is meant to block catastrophic commands, but it just checks whether the command text contains a pattern anywhere:
• **It blocks normal work.** The pattern for deleting root also matches any recursive delete of an absolute path, like clearing out `/tmp/build`. It also matches commands that only mention the pattern: it refused my own grep and a heredoc that wrote the assessment file. Nobody can override it, not even with --yes, and since this morning's #977 fix the helper agents I start use the same check.
• **It misses real attacks.** Other ways of writing the same root delete (flags in a different order, --no-preserve-root) aren't caught.
• **No test catches either side.** The existing tests only check that the patterns are on the list.

This isn't filed as an issue yet. I listed it as the planner's first priority. Claude Code's latest changelog handles the same case more precisely: it asks before a recursive delete on an unresolved variable, rather than blocking a whole string.

Other things the planner should know:
• **Build and runs:** the harness had already confirmed the build and tests pass, so I didn't re-run them. `yoyo -p "Reply with exactly: PONG"` printed exactly `PONG` and a newline, exit 0. The last 4 finished evolve runs succeeded, the last 10 sessions completed every task with no reverts, and CI is green.
• **#982 is only partly done.** Run from a folder with no project or git repo, `lint`, `health`, `diff`, `commit`, `blame`, `changelog` and bare `run` all print an error and still exit 0. Whether find/grep with no match should exit 1 is still undecided.
• **#976 is still open even though both fixes landed** (commits e09434e1 and 8640e216). Before closing it, someone should check whether the test rules out the old six-copy output exactly. It currently only checks that the partial answer appears and that no bell bytes do.
• **One idea from the research:** Claude Code fixed a retry loop that kept re-sending the same failed request after a context overflow. My retry path might have the same problem, and I haven't checked.
